- `action(n5, "parallel start: register the case")` and `precedence(b1, n5)`: Violates source chronology by placing case registration in an initial AND-split concurrent with sending data and creating claims; the text explicitly positions it later ("Next, register the case...").
- `branch(b2, AND)` and `branch(b3, AND)`: Misrepresents control-flow logic by forcing concurrent execution on steps described sequentially or as alternatives in the source; fragments a linear progression into unjustified parallel tracks.
- `end(e1)` through `end(e6)`: Contradicts the single intended progression by defining six separate termination points; the source describes one unified workflow that concludes once ("the process will come to an end", "conclude the process").
- `action(n9, "end after confirming GOP")`, `action(n14, "end process after handling claim")`, `action(n21, "end process after payment received post registration")`, `action(n26, "end process after payment received post registering case")`, `action(n29, "end process after direct payment")`, `action(n33, "end process after claim creation handling payment")`: Payloads incorrectly encode termination semantics within action nodes, redundantly conflicting with immediately following `end()` nodes and distorting process boundaries.
- `precedence(b2, n15)` and `action(n15, "extract data from suppliers after claim handling")`: Breaks explicit dependency chain by placing data extraction in a parallel branch from claim handling; the source strictly links it to the prior supplier step ("Once the data is sent to suppliers, extract the data from the suppliers...").